{"step_index":455,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T11:49:47Z","thinking":"The certificate details reveal the domain and expiration date. Examining Apache configuration is the next logical step to understand the server's setup; `apache2ctl -S`, `httpd -S`, or `apachectl -S` commands, or directly inspecting the configuration directory, will provide virtual host information if the command-line utilities are not available. This analysis will help determine if the certificate is correctly configured.\n\n\nIdentifying the Apache configuration files is the next step. The code iterates through standard configuration directories like `/etc/apache2/sites-enabled` and `/etc/httpd/conf.d`, searching for files ending in `.conf`. Then, the main configuration files are included to ensure a complete view of the virtual host definitions. The configuration files will be parsed to extract virtual host blocks.\n\n\nThe virtual host data is extracted from the configuration file by parsing relevant directives.  Domain names and aliases are identified and extracted, handling potential variations in whitespace and quoting. SSL configurations, including certificate and key file paths, are determined by searching for specific directives within the configuration.\n\n\nThe initial playbook appears to bootstrap a new user with SSH access on all hosts before proceeding. The second phase then audits web server configurations and TLS certificates. It's crucial to confirm the implications of this setup. The bootstrap playbook, which runs first on all hosts, sets up a user that the subsequent web server audit will use. There's a potential risk if the intended target hosts are not explicitly specified, potentially impacting all servers.\n\n\nThe second phase leverages `become: true` with `ansible.builtin.sudo` to execute privileged operations, ensuring flexibility regardless of the initial connection user. It probes for installed web servers by checking for standard binary paths and then detects active services via `systemctl`. The core logic involves running a script to parse web server configurations and extract TLS certificate details, including domains, ports, SSL status, certificate paths, and expiration information.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"ssh -p 30 -o BatchMode=yes koby-bowser-ansible@172.30.39.153 'sudo python3 -c \"\nimport subprocess, re, os, json, datetime\n\ndef inspect_cert(cert_path):\n    if not cert_path or not os.path.exists(cert_path):\n        return {\\\"path\\\": cert_path, \\\"exists\\\": False, \\\"status\\\": \\\"FILE NOT FOUND\\\"}\n    try:\n        cmd = [\\\"openssl\\\", \\\"x509\\\", \\\"-in\\\", cert_path, \\\"-noout\\\", \\\"-subject\\\", \\\"-issuer\\\", \\\"-enddate\\\"]\n        out = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True).stdout\n        subject, issuer, enddate_str = \\\"\\\", \\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"\n        for line in out.splitlines():\n            if line.startswith(\\\"subject=\\\"):\n                subject = line.replace(\\\"subject=\\\", \\\"\\\").strip()\n            elif line.startswith(\\\"issuer=\\\"):\n                issuer = line.replace(\\\"issuer=\\\", \\\"\\\").strip()\n            elif line.startswith(\\\"notAfter=\\\"):\n                enddate_str = line.replace(\\\"notAfter=\\\", \\\"\\\").strip()\n        \n        days_left = None\n        status = \\\"UNKNOWN\\\"\n        if enddate_str:\n            try:\n                # e.g. Nov 15 11:48:08 2026 GMT\n                dt = datetime.datetime.strptime(enddate_str, \\\"%b %d %H:%M:%S %Y %Z\\\").replace(tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc)\n                now = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)\n                days_left = (dt - now).days\n                if days_left < 0:\n                    status = f\\\"EXPIRED ({abs(days_left)} days ago)\\\"\n                elif days_left < 30:\n                    status = f\\\"EXPIRING SOON ({days_left} days left)\\\"\n                else:\n                    status = f\\\"VALID ({days_left} days left)\\\"\n            except Exception:\n                status = \\\"PARSE ERROR\\\"\n\n        # SANs\n        sans_out = subprocess.run([\\\"openssl\\\", \\\"x509\\\", \\\"-in\\\", cert_path, \\\"-noout\\\", \\\"-ext\\\", \\\"subjectAltName\\\"], capture_output=True, text=True).stdout\n        sans = []\n        for line in sans_out.splitlines():\n            if \\\"DNS:\\\" in line:\n                for p in line.split(\\\",\\\"):\n                    if \\\"DNS:\\\" in p:\n                        sans.append(p.strip().replace(\\\"DNS:\\\", \\\"\\\"))\n\n        return {\n            \\\"path\\\": cert_path,\n            \\\"exists\\\": True,\n            \\\"subject\\\": subject,\n            \\\"issuer\\\": issuer,\n            \\\"expires\\\": enddate_str,\n            \\\"days_remaining\\\": days_left,\n            \\\"status\\\": status,\n            \\\"sans\\\": sans\n        }\n    except Exception as e:\n        return {\\\"path\\\": cert_path, \\\"exists\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": str(e), \\\"status\\\": \\\"ERROR\\\"}\n\nprint(json.dumps(inspect_cert(\\\"/etc/ssl/certs/postfix.pem\\\"), indent=2))\n\"'","Cwd":"/home/koby/LINUX","RunPersistent":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Running command","toolSummary":"Test inspect_cert logic in python"}}]}
